## CA trust bundle embedded into the tvc-app binary (see //go:embed in chainalysis.go).
##
## The app has exactly one TLS egress: https://public.chainalysis.com, whose
## certificate chain terminates at a Google Trust Services (GTS) root. This
## bundle is therefore pinned to the GTS roots only, instead of the full
## Mozilla store, so that no unrelated CA can issue a certificate the enclave
## would accept.
##
## Why this bundle was rebuilt (October 2026): the previous bundle was the full
## Alpine/Mozilla store (145 roots) and included the eMudhra "emSign" roots. In the
## eMudhra intermediate CA key compromise (CVE-2026-111943, reported 2026-10-17),
## eight emSign intermediate CA private keys were recoverable from their public
## certificates, letting anyone issue certificates for any hostname that chained to
## "emSign ECC Root CA - G3". Mozilla removed that root in NSS 3.131.1 (2026-10-20)
## and Chrome is removing it in Chrome Root Store 43; eMudhra announced its exit
## from public TLS issuance. All emSign roots are gone from this bundle, and the
## bundle is now limited to the roots the single egress host actually chains to.
##
## Source: Mozilla root store as published by curl (https://curl.se/ca/cacert.pem),
## "Certificate data from Mozilla as of: Tue Oct 20 13:32:40 2026 GMT" (NSS 3.131.1,
## post eMudhra/emSign incident). Only the GTS roots were extracted; certificate bytes
## are unmodified.
##
## To refresh: download the current curl cacert.pem, extract the "GTS Root R*" entries,
## confirm the SHA-256 fingerprints below against the CCADB, and re-run
## `openssl s_client -connect public.chainalysis.com:443 -CAfile ca-certificates.crt`.

## C=US, O=Google Trust Services LLC, CN=GTS Root R1
